    {
      "id": "ai_conv_1",
      "userId": "usr_elena",
      "title": "Mindful Social Platform Principles",
      "updatedAt": "2026-10-27T16:29:41.852Z",
      "messages": [
        {
          "id": "ai_m1",
          "role": "user",
          "text": "What are three core guidelines for designing a social interface that reduces cognitive fatigue?",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-26T16:22:40.808Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "ai_m2",
          "role": "model",
          "text": "Here are three foundational principles for low-fatigue social design:\n\n1. **Finite Consumption Horizons**: Replace bottomless infinite scroll with clear pagination or \"You are caught up\" milestones that allow the brain to find natural closure.\n\n2. **Quiet Metadata Architecture**: Strip loud badges, high-contrast notification counters, and pulsing indicators. Keep dates and tags unboxed with subtle typography.\n\n3. **Asynchronous Intent**: Provide deliberate composition tools rather than ephemeral pressure, encouraging thoughtful longer reflections over rapid-fire reactions.",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-26T16:22:42.808Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "aim_214336f0716c",
          "role": "user",
          "text": "How should a writer approach editing their first book of essays?",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-27T16:29:36.418Z"
        },
        {
          "id": "aim_dc9e379f2326",
          "role": "model",
          "text": "Here is a perspective for your draft, Elena Vance:\n\nFocus on the single observation that surprised you first. When we write for deliberate platforms, clarity outlasts cleverness.\n\n*Suggested opening:* \"Often we mistake activity for progress. The most enduring work happens when we remove the noise and leave space for the core idea to speak for itself.\"\n\nHow does this resonance feel with what you had in mind?",
          "timestamp": "2026-10-27T16:29:41.852Z"
        }
      ]
    }
  ],
